Throughout, $\Aa$ denotes the completed ladder algebra of the singular locus: at a single point of the blown-up singular boundary $E \cong \R \times S^{2}$ the algebra generated by the Rule 1 pair $(v, v^{*})$ with
\begin{equation}
v^{2} = (v^{*})^{2} = 0, \qquad v v^{*} + v^{*} v = 1 ,
\end{equation}
so that $\Aa \cong \Mat_2(\C)$ as a factor, and over all of $E$ the CAR algebra generated by one such pair per point \cite[Thm.~8.7, Thm.~8.12]{ButlerT4}. We write $N = v^{*}v$ for the number operator, $P = N$ for the occupation projector, $\sigma = v^{*}v - vv^{*} = 2N - 1$ for the modular bivector of \cite[Thm.~6.3]{ButlerT5}, and $K = \sigma/2 = N - \tfrac12$ for the boost generator. The descent is
\begin{equation}
E(x) \;=\; PxP + (1-P)\,x\,(1-P)
\end{equation}
of \cite[Prop.~5.5]{ButlerT5}, and $\EN$ its multi-mode composition \cite[Def.~9.1]{ButlerT5}. Mode labels $k$ run over the ledger; $\omega_k > 0$ is the mode's boost weight. All statements about $\Aa$ at a single point are finite dimensional and are proved outright; the statements that need the infinite system are flagged where they occur. Units and curvature conventions are T5's.

Let $\sigma_r$ be the unit boost bivector of the radial plane and $I$ the pseudoscalar. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $(I\sigma_r)^{2} = -1$, and $\{\sigma_r,\, I\sigma_r\}$ is precisely the doubled eigenplane pair of the shape operator, $S(B) = -2B$ on it, of \cite[Thm.~6.1]{ButlerT5};
\item the rotor $R_\theta := \exp(\theta\, I\sigma_r/2) = \cos(\theta/2) + I\sigma_r \sin(\theta/2)$ satisfies $R_{2\pi} = -1$ and $R_{4\pi} = +1$, so the rotor has period $4\pi$ while its conjugation action $x \mapsto R_\theta x R_\theta^{-1}$ has period $2\pi$;
\item $R_\theta$ is the analytic continuation of the boost rotor $\exp(\lambda\sigma_r/2)$ under $\lambda \mapsto i\theta$ in the sense that both are one-parameter rotor flows in the same eigenplane pair, the hyperbolic one generated by the member with square $+1$ and the elliptic one by the member with square $-1$.
\item On spinors, each chiral half: with the unit of the continuation $\lambda \mapsto i\theta$ read as the conformal spinors' unit $I_{6} = I_{4}E$ (with $I_{4} = I$ and $E = e_{+}e_{-}$ the bivector of the added plane), the continued boost acts on the two chiral halves of the conformal spinors as $R_\theta$ and $R_{-\theta}$, the sense fixed by the chirality (Proposition \claimref{synthesis.wick-rotor-chiral-angular-rotor}(ii)).
\end{enumerate}
In its action on spinors, on each chiral half, the Euclidean rotation whose angle carries the periodicity is therefore not an imported device: it is the second member of the pair that Step 4b's curvature equation already selects, and it is distinguished from the boost by exactly the sign of its square, the same criterion \cite[Cor.~6.4]{ButlerT5} uses to pick out $\sigma_r$. On vectors, and for every holonomy of a real connection, it is not: there $R_\theta$ rotates along the bifurcation sphere, not around it, and the Euclidean rotation around the sphere is realised by no real rotor of $\Cl(1,3)$ (Proposition \claimref{singularity.euclidean-rotor-where-it-lies}(3)).

Let $\omega$ be a faithful state on the mode factor $\Aa \cong \Mat_2(\C)$, with density matrix $\rho = \operatorname{diag}(\lambda_0^{2}, \lambda_1^{2})$, $\lambda_i > 0$. Then in the GNS representation of $\omega$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the representation space is $\C^{2}\otimes\C^{2}$, the cyclic vector is $\Omega = \sum_i \lambda_i |ii\rangle$, and $\Omega$ is cyclic and separating for $\pi(\Aa) = \Aa \otimes 1$;
\item the commutant $\pi(\Aa)' = 1 \otimes \Aa$ is the partner copy, and is generated from $\pi(\Aa)$ by the modular conjugation, $\pi(\Aa)' = J\pi(\Aa)J$;
\item any two purifications of $\omega$ are related by a unitary acting on the purifying factor alone, so $\Omega$ is unique up to a unitary in the commutant;
\item when $\omega$ is the Gibbs state at $\beta$, $\Omega$ is the thermofield double, $\lambda_0 = \cos\theta$, $\lambda_1 = \sin\theta$ with $\tan\theta = e^{-\omega_k/2T}$, which is exactly the state produced by \cite[Lem.~7.2]{ButlerT5}.
\end{enumerate}
Consequently the global purity of hypothesis (i), the existence of the partner, and the thermofield form of the global state are consequences of the one-sided state, not independent choices; and the ``two ends of the pair'' of Step 4d are the algebra and its commutant in the state's own GNS representation.

Let $\sigma = e_0e_1$ be the boost bivector of the hyperbolic atom in the radial plane, with $\sigma^{2} = +1$, and let $\Aa \cong \Mat_2(\C)$ be the mode algebra, the complexification of the atom.
\begin{enumerate}
\item The boost rotor $R_\lambda = \exp(\lambda\sigma/2)$ continues to imaginary rapidity $\lambda = i\theta$ as $R_{i\theta} = \exp(i\theta\sigma/2) = \cos(\theta/2) + i\sigma\sin(\theta/2)$, a unitary of $\Aa$ whose generator $i\sigma$ squares to $-1$; $R_{2\pi i} = -1$ and $R_{4\pi i} = +1$, so the rotor has period $4\pi$ and its conjugation action period $2\pi$.
\item On the two-dimensional module on which $v, v^{*}$ act as ladder operators, $R_{i\theta} = \operatorname{diag}(e^{-i\theta/2}, e^{i\theta/2})$: a rotation through $2\pi$ multiplies every spinor by $-1$, while its conjugation action on $\Aa$ is the identity.
\item The generator $i\sigma$ is not an element of the real algebra $\Cl(1,3)$. The dual member of the curvature-selected pair, $I\sigma_r = e_0e_1e_2e_3\,e_0e_1 = e_2e_3$, is the \emph{angular} plane: its rotor $\exp(\theta I\sigma_r/2)$ fixes $e_0$, $e_1$ and $\sigma_r$ and rotates $e_2$ into $e_3$, a rotation along the bifurcation sphere, not around it. The Euclidean rotation around the bifurcation sphere is therefore realised by no real rotor of $\Cl(1,3)$, and in particular not by the curvature-selected pair $\{\sigma_r, I\sigma_r\}$; it is realised by the unitary $R_{i\theta}$ of the complexified radial plane.
\end{enumerate}
